# Edge function secrets — copy to `supabase/.env`, paste your values, push once.
#
#   cp supabase/.env.example supabase/.env
#   # paste the real values in
#   npm run secrets:push          # supabase secrets set --env-file supabase/.env
#   npm run secrets:list          # confirm what the project actually holds
#
# `supabase/.env` is gitignored. These are SERVER-side secrets: they are read by
# Deno inside Supabase's runtime (`Deno.env.get`), never by the app.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why these are NOT in .env, and NOT in EAS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EAS environments hold variables for an app BUILD. Nothing in a build can
# reach an edge function's environment, so a Resend key set in EAS would never
# be read by anything — the function would go on returning `emailed: false`.
#
# The failure mode of getting this wrong is not "it doesn't work". Anything
# named `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` is INLINED INTO THE APK by Metro, and an APK can be
# unzipped by anyone who installs it. `RESEND_API_KEY` is a credential to send
# mail as your domain — leaking it means a stranger sends mail as you, and the
# resulting spam complaints land on your sending reputation. Never give any
# name in this file an `EXPO_PUBLIC_` prefix, and never copy one into `.env`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do not add SUPABASE_* here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `SUPABASE_URL`, `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` and `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` are
# injected into every function automatically. The prefix is also reserved —
# `supabase secrets set` REJECTS any name starting with `SUPABASE_`, so adding
# one here makes the whole push fail rather than just that line.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Group invitations — supabase/functions/send-invite
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resend → API Keys. Sending permission is enough; this never reads anything.
#
# Until this is a real key the invite flow still WORKS — the function creates a
# redeemable invitation and returns the link, and the members screen opens the
# share sheet instead of claiming an email went out. So a wrong value here is
# invisible unless you look: it degrades, it does not break. Check with
# `npm run secrets:list`, and see docs/OPERATIONS.md §5.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_REPLACE_WITH_RESEND_API_KEY

# The From: header. The domain must be VERIFIED in Resend with SPF and DKIM
# published, or invitations are accepted and then filed as spam — which looks
# identical to success from the app. Resend → Domains.
#
# ⚠ "Daykeep <onboarding@resend.dev>" is Resend's sandbox sender. It needs no
# domain and DELIVERS ONLY to the address that owns the Resend account: every
# other recipient is rejected after the send is accepted. It is the right value
# while testing the wiring and it cannot ship — with it set, signup codes,
# password resets and invitations all fail for real users while working
# perfectly for you. `npm run configure:resend -- --env <env>` says so on every
# run that finds it.
#
# This is also the sender Auth uses, unless AUTH_SMTP_FROM overrides it.
# Defaults to "Daykeep <invites@daykeep.app>" when unset.
INVITE_FROM=Daykeep <invites@daykeep.app>

# Where an invitation link points. The token is appended: <base>/<token>.
#
# Must be an **https** URL, and the thing it points at must be the `join` edge
# function (or something that behaves like it). A `daykeep://` value here looks
# like it should work and does not: Gmail strips custom schemes out of links
# entirely, and on a phone without the app installed the scheme resolves to
# nothing — which is most of the people an invitation is sent to.
#
# Defaults to this project's own function, "<SUPABASE_URL>/functions/v1/join",
# which is correct for every project without being configured. Set it only to
# point at a real website once one exists.
#   supabase functions deploy join --no-verify-jwt
APP_INVITE_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The invitation landing page — supabase/functions/join
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deep-link scheme THIS project's app builds use. Staging builds are
# `daykeep-staging` (scripts/build-env.js gives them their own scheme so both
# apps can sit on one phone), so the staging project must be set to that or its
# invitations open the production app — which then rejects a token its own
# database has never seen.
#
# Defaults to "daykeep", which is the right thing to be wrong about: a
# production project with this unset still works.
APP_LINK_SCHEME=daykeep

# Store pages, shown on the landing page to somebody who does not have the app.
# Both optional: unset renders the page with no store buttons rather than with
# buttons that 404, which is correct until Daykeep is actually published.
ANDROID_STORE_URL=
IOS_STORE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications — supabase/functions/notify-group and notify-album
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expo access token, used to send through Expo's push service.
# expo.dev → Account Settings → Access Tokens.
#
# Optional in the same shape as the Resend key: without it the functions still
# return successfully and the write they accompany still succeeds — the
# notification is simply never delivered. Nothing surfaces that in the app.
EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — supabase/functions/safepay-checkout, safepay-webhook,
#            safepay-cancel-subscription
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Safepay dashboard → Developers → API Keys. Use the SANDBOX keys until you
# have tested a real subscription end to end; the sandbox and production keys
# are different values against different hosts, and the SDK picks the host from
# SAFEPAY_ENVIRONMENT rather than from the key.
#
# These do NOT degrade the way the Resend and Expo keys above do. `safepayClient()`
# throws "SAFEPAY_API_KEY / SAFEPAY_SECRET_KEY are not configured" when either is
# missing, so checkout returns a 500 and the user sees a failed purchase rather
# than a quietly skipped one. Placeholder values do not help either: they get past
# the configuration check and then fail at Safepay's API, which is a slower and
# more confusing way to arrive at the same place.

# 'sandbox' or 'production'. Defaults to 'sandbox' when unset, which is the safe
# default but also means a missing value here silently points production traffic
# at the sandbox host — set it explicitly per project.
SAFEPAY_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# The public API key. Sandbox keys and production keys are not interchangeable.
SAFEPAY_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_SAFEPAY_SANDBOX_API_KEY

# The v1 secret. This signs requests as your merchant account: a leak lets a
# stranger take payments as you. Never give it an EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix, and never
# copy it into the app's .env — see the warning at the top of this file.
SAFEPAY_SECRET_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_SAFEPAY_SANDBOX_SECRET_KEY

# Verifies that a webhook really came from Safepay. Without it the webhook
# handler cannot distinguish a genuine payment notification from a forged one,
# which is how a subscription gets activated without anybody paying for it.
SAFEPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=REPLACE_WITH_SAFEPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET

# Where Safepay hands the customer back after paying. Must match app.json's
# scheme — the OS routes on that, so a stale scheme drops the customer nowhere
# after a successful payment. Defaults to 'daykeep://billing/callback'.
SAFEPAY_RETURN_BASE_URL=daykeep://billing/callback

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploying the functions that read these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   supabase link --project-ref <ref>     # once per machine
#   supabase functions deploy send-invite
#   supabase functions deploy join --no-verify-jwt
#   supabase functions deploy notify-group
#   supabase functions deploy notify-album
#   supabase functions deploy safepay-checkout
#   supabase functions deploy safepay-webhook
#   supabase functions deploy safepay-cancel-subscription
#
# `join` is the only one deployed with --no-verify-jwt, and it has to be: it is
# the https page an invitation email links to, opened by somebody who has no
# account yet and therefore no JWT to present. It reads nothing and writes
# nothing — see the header of supabase/functions/join/index.ts.
#
# Secrets and code deploy separately: pushing a secret does NOT redeploy a
# function, and deploying a function does NOT carry secrets with it. A function
# picks up a changed secret on its next cold start.
